Single private primitive, three public wrappers:

```rust
fn leaf_for(&self, i: usize) -> &[T] {          // caller guarantees i < size
    let to = self.tail_off();
    if i >= to { return &self.tail; }           // tail region
    let mut node: &Node<T> = &self.root;
    let mut level = self.shift;
    while level > 0 {
        match node { Branch(ch) => node = &ch[(i >> level) & MASK],
                     Leaf(_)    => break }      // unreachable; degrade instead of UB
        level -= BITS;
    }
    match node { Leaf(v) => v, Branch(_) => &[] }
}
pub fn get(&self, i: usize) -> Option<&T> {
    if i >= self.size { return None; }
    self.leaf_for(i).get(i & MASK)              // total: no unwrap, no unchecked index
}
```
- `pub fn get(&self, i) -> Option<&T>` — total, never panics. This is the function every internal caller uses.
- `impl Index<usize> for Vector<T>` — `operator[]` equivalent; on out-of-bounds it panics with the idiomatic message `index out of bounds: the len is {size} but the index is {i}`. C++'s `immer` `operator[]` on OOB is UB; a deterministic panic is a strict improvement and is the documented contract. The driver never hits it (it iterates `0..v.len()`).
- `pub fn first()/last()` delegate to `get` (`last` guards `size == 0` before `size - 1` — an unguarded `size - 1` would underflow-panic in debug and wrap in release).
- Lifetimes: all returned references are reborrows through `&'a Rc<Node<T>>` → `&'a Node<T>`, so `&'a T` is sound with zero `unsafe`. The whole module is `unsafe`-free.
- Cost: 1 shift/mask per level, ≤ 5 levels at n = 10^7, ≤ 13 worst case. No allocation, no recursion (the descent is a `while` loop, so it is depth-safe by construction even if `shift` were corrupted).

input.iteration_algorithm:
Chunk-caching forward cursor — O(1) amortized per element, zero `unsafe`, no per-element tree descent:

```rust
pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    vec: &'a Vector<T>,
    idx: usize,          // next index to yield
    leaf: &'a [T],       // cached chunk covering [leaf_base, leaf_base + leaf.len())
    leaf_base: usize,    // multiple of WIDTH; starts at usize::MAX so the first next() always refreshes
}
impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        if self.idx >= self.vec.size { return None; }
        if self.leaf_base != (self.idx & !MASK) {          // crossed a 32-boundary
            self.leaf_base = self.idx & !MASK;
            self.leaf = self.vec.leaf_for(self.idx);
        }
        let out = self.leaf.get(self.idx - self.leaf_base); // Option, never indexed unchecked
        self.idx += 1;
        out
    }
    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) { let n = self.vec.size - self.idx; (n, Some(n)) }
}
impl<'a, T> ExactSizeIterator for Iter<'a, T> {}
```
- Correct for the tail because `tail_off` is a multiple of WIDTH, so `idx & !MASK` is the right base in both the trie and the tail region. This is exactly the invariant to state in a comment above the `&!MASK`.
- One tree descent per 32 elements → ~312 500 descents for 10^7 elements, negligible; `count`, `nth`, `last`, `sum`, `collect` inherit correct default impls from `size_hint` + `ExactSizeIterator`.
- `pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T>` plus `impl<'a, T> IntoIterator for &'a Vector<T> { type Item = &'a T; type IntoIter = Iter<'a, T>; }` so `for x in &v` works — this is the direct translation of C++ `for (auto x : v)` (note C++ `auto x` copies; `&T` + explicit deref is the faithful, allocation-free analogue).
- Also provide by-value `impl<T: Clone> IntoIterator for Vector<T>` (holds the `Vector` + an index, yields `self.get(i).cloned()`), so `for x in v` also compiles. Requires `T: Clone` — document it.
- `DoubleEndedIterator`: implement `next_back` with the SIMPLE non-caching form (`self.end -= 1; self.vec.get(self.end)`, O(log32 n) per step) rather than maintaining a second cached chunk. Correctness over micro-speed; a shared `idx`/`end` pair with two caches is a classic off-by-one farm.
